From 2af2d359caf09a57e6b7fafbdbabd9c7b552b100 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dnz3d4c <dnz3d4c@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 07:56:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] Korean: the mod's own strings, generated from the
 catalogue
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The subject stays put on purpose - this one commit holds every translated
string and grows with each feature group, so the patch file keeps its name
instead of being renamed on every batch.

Generated, not written. The Korean here comes from overlay/ko/ko.json through
tools/ko-apply; this C# is output. Do not hand-edit it, and do not resolve
conflicts in it - drop the commit and run the tool again against the new tree.

That is the point of the commit. Korean has to land on 688 lines of a file
upstream touches in nearly every release, and upstream released seven times in
eight days. A hand-written diff of that size would have to be re-merged by hand
on that cadence, by someone reading the diff with a screen reader. Regenerating
is deterministic; merging is not. The failure mode becomes "N strings in the
catalogue are no longer in the source", which names the sentence that moved.

80 strings on 88 call sites so far: the six language-switch lines, and now the
chat group - the settings menu, the buffer names, the tab and channel rows, the
history, and the channel prefixes spoken in front of every incoming line.

The channel names are the game's own, read out of the Korean client's Addon
sheet rather than guessed (tools/ko-terms, and overlay/ko/terms.json records the
row number for each). The list at rows 645-655 is where they come from: 말하기,
외치기, 떠들기, 파티, 연합 파티, 귓속말, 자유부대, 혼잣말.

Two of them are worth stating because guessing would have gone wrong.

- The game has no word "채팅" - zero hits across all 19,592 Addon rows. It says
  로그 for the log and 대화 for conversation, so the mod says that too.
- Echo is 혼잣말, not a transliteration.

One line changes word order. ChatFromLine was "{prefix} from {sender}: {message}"
and the Korean is "{sender} {prefix}: {message}", which is how the sentence has
to run in Korean - "모모디 말하기: ...". The placeholder names are untouched;
only their order moved, and the tool checks that the set is identical so a
dropped slot cannot slip through.

German and English wording is byte-for-byte what it was. The tool only appends a
third argument; it never reads or rewrites the first two.

Build: 0 warnings, 0 errors against ClientStructs 7.55 and 7.51
Verified: DE/EN snapshot unchanged - 688 pairs identical after moving 82 sites
off the ternary, which is what the snapshot exists to prove. Round trip checked:
regenerating from the parent commit reproduces this tree exactly
In-Game: not checked - the chat group needs listening. The language switch was
confirmed earlier and is unchanged here

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
